{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'gh pr view 1088 --repo endojs/endo-but-for-bots --json number,title,url,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRepositoryOwner,baseRefName,author,body,commits,statusCheckRollup'","aggregated_output":"{\"author\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},\"baseRefName\":\"llm\",\"body\":\"Standing pull request for the continuous **Ironhorse fuzz** campaign: each\\nreproduced finding amends this one PR with a load-bearing regression case and\\nits causal fix. Base `llm`. Adopted by the durable marker below.\\n\\n<!-- garden-job: ironhorse-fuzz-findings -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `493390fc03979205` — target `differential_regexp_surface`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 4-byte minimized input `08 74 74 2a` (sha256\\n`450a95b7…dabbcf7`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into a deeply\\nnested `new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"s\\\").toString()` whose rendered source is **1099\\nbytes**. The target panicked with a *result* divergence vs the XS pin.\\n\\n**Root cause (oracle-side, not a port defect).** The XS differential oracle\\ncaptured the completion value into a fixed **1024-byte** buffer\\n(`ENDOR_RESULT_MAX`) and `strncpy`-truncated it to 1023 bytes. The port\\n(`ironhorse`) rendered the whole 1099-byte string **correctly**, so the harness\\ncompared the oracle's truncated 1023-byte prefix against the port's full result\\nand reported a false divergence. Any program whose completion value exceeds the\\nbuffer was a latent false positive of this class.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, oracle-side).**\\n- Grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to **16 KiB** so realistic completion values compare\\n  in full instead of at a 1 KiB cliff.\\n- Record the untruncated byte length (`result_len`) at both capture sites and\\n  surface `OracleOutcome::result_truncated`. When a value still overflows the\\n  buffer, `differential_check_with_symbols` / `differential_check_result_only`\\n  **skip honestly** (like an out-of-subset program) instead of comparing a\\n  truncated prefix — so the class can never spuriously diverge again, at any\\n  size.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 4-byte input through the real differential\\n  check and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if the oracle fix is\\n  reverted).\\n- `xs-oracle`: a 2000-char completion value is captured **untruncated** and not\\n  flagged truncated.\\n- `ironhorse-vm`: replays the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols\\n  (**no XS oracle / no `c/moddable` submodule**) and asserts the port renders\\n  the whole 1099-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\n**Note (latent twin).** `ModuleRunOutcome` shares the same fixed result buffer;\\nthe C side now records `result_len` on that path too, but the module differential\\ntargets do not yet consult a `result_truncated` flag. Out of scope for this\\nfinding; worth a follow-up if a module fuzz target ever surfaces a >16 KiB result.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` — target `differential_source`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 5-byte minimized input `2d 57 27 48 86` (sha256\\n`749f2021…e09a4f9`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into the\\narithmetic program\\n\\n```js\\n((729808896 && (729808896 && (-83 && 327155712)))\\n * (((-83 && 327155712) * (729808896 % 603979776)) % 729808896))\\n```\\n\\nThe target panicked with a *result* divergence:\\n`oracle=\\\"57632001481506816\\\"` vs `ironhorse=\\\"57632001481506820\\\"`.\\n\\n**Root cause (harness-side, not a port defect).** In IEEE-754 doubles\\n(ECMAScript Number semantics) the program evaluates to\\n`327155712 * 176160768` = `819 · 2^46`, the **exactly-representable** double\\nwhose real value is `57632001481506816`. Both engines compute the *identical*\\ndouble — they differ only in how they render it to a string:\\n\\n- XS's `fx_dtoa` (xsdtoa.c) prints the double's exact integer,\\n  `57632001481506816` (17 significant digits).\\n- ironhorse — like V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's Number\\n  `toString` (\\\"let *k* be as small as possible\\\") — prints the **shortest**\\n  round-tripping decimal, `57632001481506820` (16 significant digits).\\n\\nBoth spellings parse back to the same double, so the port was never wrong;\\nironhorse is the spec-conformant one (verified against Node/V8, which prints\\n`57632001481506820`). The harness compared the raw completion strings\\nbyte-for-byte and read XS's non-shortest rendering as a value divergence — the\\nsame *harness-artifact, not engine bug* shape as findings `493390fc03979205`\\nand `5d122a6fc10babd9`.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, harness-side).** Compare **Number** completions by their\\nIEEE-754 double rather than their decimal spelling. A new `results_agree`\\nhelper treats two result strings as equal when they are byte-identical **or**\\nboth parse as the same finite `f64`; the three differential checks\\n(`differential_check`, `…_with_symbols`, `…_result_only`) call it at their\\nresult-comparison site. Two *different* doubles never share a parse, so every\\ngenuine value divergence is still caught; only differently-spelled renderings\\nof the *same* double are suppressed. Non-numeric completions (booleans,\\nstrings, `Infinity`/`NaN`) fall through to the byte comparison unchanged.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 5-byte input through the real\\n  `differential_check` and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if\\n  the fix is reverted), plus a `results_agree` unit test pinning the\\n  same-double / different-double boundary.\\n- `ironhorse-vm` (`finding_d99d263fcf6ca7a7_large_integer_dtoa`): pins the\\n  port's `number_to_ecma_string` for the reproducing value to the shortest\\n  round-tripping decimal `57632001481506820` (matching V8, not XS's exact\\n  integer). Builds **without the XS oracle or the `c/moddable` submodule**, and\\n  is the crate CI actually runs (`cargo test -p ironhorse-vm`).\\n\",\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T05:17:52Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T05:17:52Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…zz 493390fc03979205)\\n\\nThe continuous Ironhorse fuzz target `differential_regexp_surface` reproduced a\\ncrash on the 4-byte input `08 74 74 2a`, which the structure-aware generator\\nfolds into a deeply nested `new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"s\\\").toString()`. The rendered\\nRegExp source is 1099 bytes.\\n\\nThe XS differential oracle captured the completion value into a fixed 1024-byte\\nbuffer (`ENDOR_RESULT_MAX`), truncating it to 1023 bytes with `strncpy`. The\\nport (ironhorse) rendered the whole 1099-byte string correctly, so the harness\\nread the oracle's truncated prefix as a result divergence and panicked. The\\nport was never wrong; the defect was oracle-side.\\n\\nFix (oracle-side, minimal):\\n- Grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to 16 KiB so realistic completion values compare in\\n  full instead of at a 1 KiB cliff.\\n- Record the untruncated byte length (`result_len`) at both result-capture\\n  sites and surface `OracleOutcome::result_truncated`. When a value still\\n  overflows the buffer the differential checks skip honestly — exactly like an\\n  out-of-subset program — instead of comparing a truncated prefix, so the class\\n  can never spuriously diverge again regardless of buffer size.\\n\\nRegressions (finding 493390fc03979205):\\n- ironhorse-fuzz: replay the exact input through the real differential check and\\n  assert no divergence (load-bearing; fails if the oracle fix is reverted).\\n- xs-oracle: a 2000-char completion value is captured untruncated and not\\n  flagged truncated.\\n- ironhorse-vm: replay the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols (no XS\\n  oracle / no c/moddable submodule) and assert the port renders the whole\\n  1099-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(xs-oracle): stop truncating the differential completion value (fu…\",\"oid\":\"7fae4aea2f67f5b28c9ab0e371f95c0c6f25ab15\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T05:21:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T05:21:09Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…fuzz 5d122a6fc10babd9)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_regexp` arm flagged a \\\"match\\nmeter\\\" divergence on a 3-byte input (sha256 79942bf9…d7a917) that folds\\ninto a deeply nested empty-matchable pattern over \\\"00b00\\\"@2. Matching it\\ndispatches 243671 metered steps, so the raw 16.16 match meter is\\n243671*65536 = 15_969_222_656 — past 2^32.\\n\\nBoth engines dispatch the identical step histogram (verified op-for-op),\\nso the port's meter was never wrong. XS's own `meterIndex` is a txU8\\n(xsAll.h); the port meters into a `u64` and holds the full value. The\\noracle shim, however, copied that meter into a 32-bit `txU4` field and so\\nwrapped it to 15_969_222_656 mod 2^32 = 3_084_320_768 — a phantom\\ndivergence against the port's correct figure. Same shape as the prior\\nstanding finding (493390fc03979205): a harness-side truncation, not an\\nengine bug.\\n\\nFix (oracle-side only): widen the regexp meter fields — compile/match\\nraw meters and computrons — to 64 bit end to end (EndorRegExpResult in\\nxs_shim.c and XsRegExpResultRaw/RegExpOutcome in lib.rs), and drop the now\\n-redundant `as u64` casts at the two comparison sites.\\n\\nRegressions:\\n- xs-oracle unit test `regexp_match_meter_is_not_truncated_to_32_bits`:\\n  asserts the oracle reports the full 15_969_222_656 (fails pre-fix with\\n  the wrapped value). Needs the submodule, like the sibling oracle tests.\\n- ironhorse-vm `finding_5d122a6fc10babd9_regexp_meter_overflow`: replays\\n  the exact case through the port's `compile`+`match_regexp`, asserting no\\n  panic and the full-width meter (> u32::MAX). Builds without the oracle\\n  or the c/moddable submodule, per the standing-branch convention.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(xs-oracle): stop truncating the differential regexp match meter (…\",\"oid\":\"c8497fd88b2414a409cec80f47e51800e053366a\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:05:22Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:05:22Z\",\"messageBody\":\"… spelling (fuzz d99d263fcf6ca7a7)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_source` arm flagged a \\\"result\\\"\\ndivergence on the 5-byte input `2d 57 27 48 86` (sha256 749f2021…e09a4f9),\\nwhich the structure-aware generator folds into the arithmetic program\\n\\n    ((729808896 && (729808896 && (-83 && 327155712)))\\n     * (((-83 && 327155712) * (729808896 % 603979776)) % 729808896))\\n\\nIn IEEE-754 doubles (ECMAScript Number semantics) this evaluates to\\n`327155712 * 176160768` = `819 * 2^46`, the *exactly*-representable double\\nwhose real value is `57632001481506816`. Both engines compute the identical\\ndouble — they diverged only in how they render it to a string:\\n\\n  - XS's `fx_dtoa` (xsdtoa.c) printed the double's exact integer,\\n    `57632001481506816` (17 significant digits).\\n  - ironhorse — like V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's Number\\n    `toString` (\\\"let k be as small as possible\\\") — printed the *shortest*\\n    round-tripping decimal, `57632001481506820` (16 significant digits).\\n\\nBoth spellings parse back to the same double, so the port was never wrong;\\nironhorse is the spec-conformant one (verified against Node/V8). The harness,\\ncomparing the raw completion strings byte-for-byte, read XS's non-shortest\\nrendering as a value divergence. Same shape as the prior standing findings\\n(493390fc03979205, 5d122a6fc10babd9): a harness-side rendering artifact, not\\nan engine bug.\\n\\nFix (harness-side, minimal): compare Number completions by their IEEE-754\\ndouble rather than their decimal spelling. A new `results_agree` helper treats\\ntwo result strings as equal when they are byte-identical OR both parse as the\\nsame finite f64; the three differential checks (`differential_check`, `…\\n_with_symbols`, `…_result_only`) use it at their result-comparison site. Two\\n*different* doubles never share a parse, so every genuine value divergence is\\nstill caught; only differently-spelled renderings of the *same* double are\\nsuppressed. Non-numeric completions (booleans, strings, `Infinity`/`NaN`)\\nfall through to the byte comparison unchanged.\\n\\nRegressions (finding d99d263fcf6ca7a7):\\n- ironhorse-fuzz: replay the exact 5-byte input through the real\\n  `differential_check` and assert no divergence (load-bearing; fails if the\\n  fix is reverted), plus a `results_agree` unit test pinning the\\n  same-double/different-double boundary.\\n- ironhorse-vm `finding_d99d263fcf6ca7a7_large_integer_dtoa`: pin the port's\\n  `number_to_ecma_string` for the reproducing value to the shortest\\n  round-tripping decimal `57632001481506820` (matching V8, not XS's exact\\n  integer). Builds without the XS oracle or the c/moddable submodule, per the\\n  standing-branch convention, and is the crate CI actually runs\\n  (`cargo test -p ironhorse-vm`).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(ironhorse-fuzz): compare Number completions by value, not decimal…\",\"oid\":\"4ae39593bb6e125602a3054641ffde1e11501d39\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:13:55Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:14:12Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…fuzz 3ea435c58b4c588e)\\n\\nThe continuous Ironhorse fuzz target `differential_regexp_surface` reproduced a\\ncrash on the 4-byte input `8c 8c 8c a2` (sha256\\n9df4e2b4ff1278d84c09d3caad69d47b90401dae21573f6d581a7085716e1638), which the\\nstructure-aware generator folds into a deeply nested\\n`new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"s\\\").toString()`. The completion value is a 1128-byte\\nstring.\\n\\nThis is a distinct minimized input of the SAME class as finding\\n`493390fc03979205`: the pre-fix XS differential oracle captured the completion\\nvalue into a fixed 1024-byte buffer and truncated it to 1023 bytes, so the\\nharness read the port's *correct* full 1128-byte result as a divergence and\\npanicked. The port was never wrong. The causal defect is oracle-side and was\\nalready fixed for `493390fc03979205` (grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to 16 KiB + skip\\nhonestly when a result still overflows the buffer); at the standing branch the\\noracle now captures the full 1128-byte result (`result_truncated=false`, under\\nthe 16 KiB buffer) and both engines agree. Confirmed: the input crashes on the\\n`llm` base (old 1024-byte buffer) and checks clean on the standing branch.\\n\\nNo engine/port/oracle code changed — the fix already covers this input. This\\ncommit adds a load-bearing regression lock so the exact input stays covered:\\n\\n- ironhorse-fuzz: replay the exact 4 bytes through the real\\n  `differential_check_with_symbols` and assert no divergence (fails if the\\n  oracle buffer fix is reverted).\\n- ironhorse-vm: replay the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols (no XS\\n  oracle / no c/moddable submodule) and assert the port renders the whole\\n  1128-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock regexp .toString() truncation (…\",\"oid\":\"e29083075d477a185b0a001a492f9251f04d1076\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:21:11Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:21:18Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…15d6d93)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_source` arm flagged a \\\"result\\\"\\ndivergence on the 5-byte input `e1 1b dc dc dc` (sha256 203db557…449e2),\\nwhich the structure-aware generator folds into the arithmetic program\\n\\n    ((-(-(-226492416))) * (-(-(-226492416))))\\n\\nThe literal `226492416` is the generator's \\\"larger integer\\\" atom `27 << 23`;\\nthe triple unary minus collapses to a single negate, so the program is\\n`(-226492416) * (-226492416) = 226492416^2 = (27*2^23)^2 = 729 * 2^46`, the\\nexactly-representable double whose real value is `51298814505517056`.\\n\\nReproduced against the pinned XS oracle (moddable 23b4d6b): XS's `fx_dtoa`\\nrenders the exact integer `51298814505517056` (17 digits) while ironhorse —\\nlike V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's \\\"let k be as small as\\npossible\\\" — renders the shortest round-tripping decimal `51298814505517060`\\n(verified byte-identical to Node/V8). Both spellings parse back to the same\\ndouble, so the port was never wrong; ironhorse is the spec-conformant one.\\n\\nSame class as finding `d99d263fcf6ca7a7`: the differential harness already\\ncompares numeric completions by their IEEE-754 double rather than their\\ndecimal spelling (`results_agree`), so this input is already suppressed on the\\nstanding branch — no port or harness change is required. This change is\\nregression-only:\\n\\n  - `ironhorse-vm/tests/finding_5c29667cc15d6d93_large_integer_dtoa.rs`: the\\n    CI-visible, submodule-free regression — pins the port's own\\n    `number_to_ecma_string(729*2^46) == \\\"51298814505517060\\\"` so the port can\\n    never be \\\"corrected\\\" to XS's non-conformant exact-integer spelling. (The\\n    engine's CI runs only `cargo test -p ironhorse-vm -p ironhorse-snapshot`;\\n    the oracle-linked crates need the c/moddable submodule and are excluded.)\\n  - `ironhorse-fuzz` test `finding_5c29667cc15d6d93_large_integer_dtoa_agrees`:\\n    replays the exact bytes through `gen_program` + `differential_check` and\\n    asserts no divergence (oracle-verified; needs the submodule).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): pin large-integer dtoa spelling (fuzz 5c29667cc…\",\"oid\":\"a2733761a57ab603cf9a6869aff2f7116a8ec596\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:23:47Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:24:37Z\",\"messageBody\":\"… a136f9038a1001fb)\\n\\nThe continuous Ironhorse fuzz target `differential_regexp_surface` reproduced a\\ncrash on the 5-byte input `2c 2c 2c d4 88` (sha256\\nd3bc62680a221ff9518c4aad6b03787bded65b75091e3b1dd34b1451e7a5835c), which the\\nstructure-aware generator folds into a deeply nested\\n`new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"m\\\").source`. The completion value is a 1955-byte string.\\n\\nThis is a distinct minimized input of the SAME class as findings\\n`493390fc03979205` and `3ea435c58b4c588e`: the pre-fix XS differential oracle\\ncaptured the completion value into a fixed 1024-byte buffer and truncated it to\\n1023 bytes, so the harness read the port's *correct* full 1955-byte result as a\\ndivergence and panicked. The port was never wrong. The two prior inputs reached\\nthe long result via `.toString()`; this one reaches it via the `.source`\\naccessor (generator selector 4), a distinct fold. The causal defect is\\noracle-side and was already fixed for `493390fc03979205` (grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX`\\nto 16 KiB + skip honestly when a result still overflows the buffer); at the\\nstanding branch the oracle now captures the full 1955-byte result\\n(`result_truncated=false`, well under the 16 KiB buffer) and both engines agree.\\nConfirmed: the input crashes on the `llm` base (38ca1d189, old 1024-byte buffer)\\nwith a truncated oracle result vs the port's full 1955 bytes, and checks clean on\\nthe standing branch.\\n\\nNo engine/port/oracle code changed — the fix already covers this input. This\\ncommit adds a load-bearing regression lock so the exact input stays covered\\n(`fuzz/corpus` and `fuzz/artifacts` are gitignored, so a corpus seed is not a\\npermanent regression):\\n\\n- ironhorse-fuzz: replay the exact 5 bytes through the real\\n  `differential_check_with_symbols` and assert no divergence (fails if the\\n  oracle buffer fix is reverted).\\n- ironhorse-vm: replay the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols (no XS\\n  oracle / no c/moddable submodule) and assert the port renders the whole\\n  1955-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock regexp .source truncation (fuzz…\",\"oid\":\"cd9c3251a93960f4a5514763a8e9964c4a1daa20\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:35:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:35:59Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…3d074ec)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_source` arm flagged a \\\"result\\\"\\ndivergence on the 4-byte input `d8 7f 33 ba`\\n(sha256 6abb2fe734124222bc19e12518fa968a59f254edea3c9ed262414cb4637c736f),\\nwhich the structure-aware generator folds into the arithmetic program\\n\\n    ((~(~(1560281088 * true))) * ((~(1560281088 * true)) << ((~true) << (true << true))))\\n\\nThe literal `1560281088` is the generator's \\\"larger integer\\\" atom `186 << 23`.\\nIn ToInt32 arithmetic the left factor is `1560281088`, the outer shift count is\\n`(~true) << (true << true) = -8` (taken mod 32 → 24), the right factor is\\n`(~1560281088) << 24 = -16777216`, and the product is\\n`1560281088 * -16777216 = -(186 * 2^47) = -(93 * 2^48)`, the exactly-representable\\ndouble whose real value is `-26177172834091008` (magnitude overflows 2^53).\\n\\nReproduced against the pinned XS oracle (moddable 23b4d6b): XS's `fx_dtoa`\\nrenders the exact integer `-26177172834091008` (17 digits) while ironhorse —\\nlike V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's \\\"let k be as small as\\npossible\\\" — renders the shortest round-tripping decimal `-26177172834091010`\\n(verified byte-identical to Node/V8). Both spellings parse back to the same\\ndouble, so the port was never wrong; ironhorse is the spec-conformant one.\\n\\nSame class as findings `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` and `5c29667cc15d6d93`: the\\ndifferential harness already compares numeric completions by their IEEE-754\\ndouble rather than their decimal spelling (`results_agree`), so this input is\\nalready suppressed on the standing branch — no port or harness change is\\nrequired. This change is regression-only:\\n\\n  - `ironhorse-vm/tests/finding_7289e31013d074ec_large_integer_dtoa.rs`: the\\n    CI-visible, submodule-free regression — pins the port's own\\n    `number_to_ecma_string(-(93*2^48)) == \\\"-26177172834091010\\\"` so the port can\\n    never be \\\"corrected\\\" to XS's non-conformant exact-integer spelling. (The\\n    engine's CI runs only `cargo test -p ironhorse-vm -p ironhorse-snapshot`;\\n    the oracle-linked crates need the c/moddable submodule and are excluded.)\\n  - `ironhorse-fuzz` test `finding_7289e31013d074ec_large_integer_dtoa_agrees`:\\n    replays the exact bytes through `gen_program` + `differential_check` and\\n    asserts no divergence (oracle-verified; needs the submodule).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): pin large-integer dtoa spelling (fuzz 7289e3101…\",\"oid\":\"098f7ab017aee42f3b6ca35207200e5a71ce7746\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:41:02Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:41:07Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…flow (fuzz 407764ab1120ed1a)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_regexp` arm re-surfaced a \\\"match\\nmeter\\\" divergence on a 3-byte input (sha256 233ee70c…e42c131, bytes `14 7f 5c`)\\nthat folds into a deeply nested, backreference-heavy pattern (capturing groups\\nplus `\\\\2`, `\\\\4`, `\\\\8`, `\\\\11`, … references to optional/unset groups) with the\\n`s` flag over \\\"abbab\\\"@1. Matching it dispatches 330041 metered steps, so the raw\\n16.16 match meter is 330041*65536 = 21_629_566_976 — past 2^32.\\n\\nThis is the same harness-side class as finding 5d122a6fc10babd9: the port meters\\ninto a `u64` and holds the full value; the XS oracle originally copied the pin's\\n64-bit `meterIndex` into a 32-bit `txU4` field and wrapped it to\\n21_629_566_976 mod 2^32 = 154_730_496, a phantom divergence against the port's\\ncorrect figure. That oracle-side truncation was already fixed (c8497fd8, widening\\nthe regexp meter fields to 64 bit end to end); the fuzzer re-found the class only\\nbecause it fuzzes the `llm` base branch, which predates the fix, so no new engine\\nor oracle change is warranted here.\\n\\nThe case is nonetheless distinct in shape from 5d122a6fc10babd9 — that one used\\nonly non-capturing groups; this one is dominated by quantified backreferences to\\nunset captures — so it independently guards the backreference dispatch path\\nagainst a silently narrowed meter.\\n\\nRegression (ironhorse-vm, builds without the oracle or the c/moddable submodule):\\n`finding_407764ab1120ed1a_regexp_meter_overflow` replays the exact generated\\npattern through the port's `compile`+`match_regexp`, asserting no panic and the\\nfull-width meter (330041*65536 > u32::MAX), pinned bit-exact so a 32-bit wrap\\nwould fail it.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock backref-heavy regexp meter over…\",\"oid\":\"410f3cb215e818c063145964389a18627fd7b2be\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:44:03Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T06:45:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…zz 783be6e6106bad98)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_source` arm flagged a \\\"result\\\"\\ndivergence on the 6-byte input `00 00 66 69 27 44` (sha256\\n95c5064e49e6c191f7f9b8be24270555d12b04c226cbc72c01406e024ff39008), which the\\nstructure-aware generator folds into\\n\\n    ((((true + 327155712) && (!true)) || ((~570425344) * (true + 327155712)))\\n     + (!((570425344 || true) + (327155712 * -128))))\\n\\nThe `&&` is `false`, so the `||` takes its right operand; the outer `!(...)` is\\n`false` and coerces to `0` under `+`. The whole program collapses to the single\\nproduct `(~570425344) * (327155712 + 1) = -570425345 * 327155713`. Its exact\\nvalue `-186617910456745985` overflows 2^53 and rounds to the double\\n`-186617910456745984`.\\n\\nReproduced against the pinned XS oracle (moddable 23b4d6b) at the fuzz project\\nSHA 38ca1d189: XS's `fx_dtoa` renders a non-shortest 17-digit form\\n`-186617910456745980` while ironhorse — like V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262\\n§6.1.6.1.20's \\\"let k be as small as possible\\\" — renders the shortest\\nround-tripping decimal `-186617910456746000` (verified byte-identical to\\nNode/V8). Both spellings parse back to the same double, so the port was never\\nwrong; ironhorse is the spec-conformant one.\\n\\nSame class as findings `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` and `5c29667cc15d6d93`: the\\ndifferential harness already compares numeric completions by their IEEE-754\\ndouble rather than their decimal spelling (`results_agree`), so this input is\\nalready suppressed on the standing branch — no port or harness change is\\nrequired. This change is regression-only:\\n\\n  - `ironhorse-vm/tests/finding_783be6e6106bad98_large_integer_dtoa.rs`: the\\n    CI-visible, submodule-free regression — pins the port's own\\n    `number_to_ecma_string(-570425345 * 327155713) == \\\"-186617910456746000\\\"`\\n    so the port can never be \\\"corrected\\\" to XS's non-shortest spelling. (The\\n    engine's CI runs only `cargo test -p ironhorse-vm -p ironhorse-snapshot`;\\n    the oracle-linked crates need the c/moddable submodule and are excluded.)\\n  - `ironhorse-fuzz` test `finding_783be6e6106bad98_large_integer_dtoa_agrees`:\\n    replays the exact 6 bytes through `gen_program` + `differential_check` and\\n    asserts no divergence (oracle-verified; needs the submodule).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock large-integer dtoa spelling (fu…\",\"oid\":\"d53328f8886b914b1130f7f1a958ab48ea912cd6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T07:01:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T07:02:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"… ab889c8f6184c60d)\\n\\nThe continuous Ironhorse fuzz target differential_regexp_surface reproduced a result divergence at the pinned llm SHA on a 4-byte minimized input (sha256 e31b5a31b...0412e07). The structure-aware generator produces a deeply nested RegExp source-accessor program whose completion value is 1216 bytes.\\n\\nThis is a distinct input of the same oracle-side class fixed by 7fae4aea2f: the old 1024-byte result buffer truncated XS output to 1023 bytes while ironhorse correctly returned the full value. The standing fix grows the buffer and skips honestly if a result still exceeds it, so no further production change is needed.\\n\\nAdd an exact-input differential regression and a submodule-free ironhorse-vm replay using frozen bytecode and symbols. Restoring the old buffer and disabling overflow detection makes the differential test fail with the original divergence; restoring the fix makes both regressions pass.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock regexp .source truncation (fuzz…\",\"oid\":\"3ef146edee61af42de2f1a7d7a68beafe57cf353\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T07:11:56Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 4.8\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T07:12:03Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…275793bca439f6e)\\n\\nThe continuous-Ironhorse-fuzz `differential_regexp` arm reported a \\\"match\\nmeter\\\" divergence on a 20-byte input (sha256 15296cb0…3e7a1e) that folds,\\nthrough `ironhorse_fuzz::gen_regexp`, into a backreference-heavy pathological\\npattern (nested empty-matchable `\\\\1*`/`\\\\3*` backrefs, lazy `\\\\1+?` /\\n`\\\\3*\\\\3*\\\\1+?` quantifiers, `{2}` repeats) over the subject \\\"0\\\"@0. The whole\\nmatch is just the leading `.` ((0,1)), but reaching it dispatches 1_120_070\\nmetered steps, so the raw 16.16 match meter is 1_120_070*65536 =\\n73_404_907_520 — 17x past 2^32.\\n\\nRoot cause: this is the SAME 32-bit-meter-truncation class as findings\\n493390fc03979205 and 5d122a6fc10babd9, already resolved on this branch by\\nwidening the XS oracle's meter fields to txU8 (commit c8497fd88). The port's\\nmatcher always metered into a u64 and held the full value; op-for-op the port\\nand the XS pin dispatch the identical 1_120_070-step histogram. Confirmed by\\nrebuilding the widened oracle and re-running the exact case: port and pin\\nagree bit-exact (matched, captures, and the full-width meter). The finding\\nonly \\\"reproduces\\\" against the base SHA 38ca1d189384245dd9accfcc2f79763a3b8ec5cb\\n(base `llm`) whose oracle predates c8497fd88 and wraps the meter to\\n73_404_907_520 mod 2^32 = 390_463_488 (5958 computrons), the phantom \\\"pin\\\"\\nvalue. No new port or oracle change is needed.\\n\\nRegression: ironhorse-vm `finding_8275793bca439f6e_regexp_meter_overflow`\\nreplays the exact generated pattern through the port's compile+match_regexp,\\nasserting no panic, the whole-match (0,1), and the full-width meter\\n(73_404_907_520 > u32::MAX, and not the 390_463_488 wrap). It is a DISTINCT,\\nbackreference-driven witness of the class (the sibling 5d122a6fc10babd9 case\\nuses only empty-star alternation, no backreferences), so it independently\\nguards the matcher against ever silently narrowing the meter. Builds without\\nthe oracle or the c/moddable submodule, per the standing-branch convention.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock regexp meter full-width (fuzz 8…\",\"oid\":\"bb42765fc1e1b6c67963f2bef5aae54de4ee84ee\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T09:34:55Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T09:34:55Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…8c673)\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock dtoa spelling (fuzz 66facfd52ae…\",\"oid\":\"13ae6c6f7b18cc405260eeb5e53a163988a4f3f4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T09:44:03Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T09:44:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…03a7b)\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock dtoa spelling (fuzz 67a52af412f…\",\"oid\":\"54089e53f394e6659cd00da8b7e0df7869dcf6bd\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T10:58:08Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"279080640+kriscendobot@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T10:58:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…2276f4edebdcb3bb)\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(ironhorse-fuzz): regression-lock regexp source truncation (fuzz …\",\"oid\":\"c2466f49c28fed0a0d210606f0319fa675080c2e\"}],\"headRefName\":\"ironhorse-fuzz-findings\",\"headRepositoryOwner\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\"},\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":1088,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33307748997/job/99247197478\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-30T10:58:26Z\",\"status\":\"IN_PROGRESS\",\"workflowName\":\"CI\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-08-30T11:01:04Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/actions/runs/33307749027/j